raw_response:
The data source is a flat ADS-B message log where each row records a timestamped sighting of an aircraft. The distinct real-world entities are: (1) the Aircraft itself, identified stably by the ICAO 24-bit address column icao24; (2) the per-row Observation event (extension class bsm:Observation) identified by the combination of icao24 and time; (3) the Location wrapper and its AbsolutePoint geometry carrying lat/lon; (4) the Velocity vector built from velocity and vertrate; (5) the Orientation built from heading; (6) the Altitude built from geoaltitude; and (7) an AlternateIdentifier for the squawk transponder code. The ontology models Location, AbsolutePoint, Velocity, Orientation, Altitude, and AlternateIdentifier as separate classes reachable via object properties from a BattlespaceConcept, so they must be emitted as their own subject_mappings rather than folded into Aircraft or Observation as literals. The Aircraft class (bsm:Aircraft) is chosen because icao24 is the standard aircraft transponder identifier and callsign is a broadcast name. The Observation class (bsm:Observation) is chosen because the schema is a timeseries of sightings. For each IRI-valued link we set the transformation string exactly equal to the referenced entity's subject_transformation: aircraft_{icao24}, obs_{icao24}_{time}, loc_{icao24}_{time}, point_{icao24}_{time}, vel_{icao24}_{time}, orient_{icao24}_{time}, alt_{icao24}_{time}, altid_{icao24}_{squawk}. Columns onground and baroaltitude have no matching ontology property (onground is not the in-service indicator; baroaltitude is a second altitude with no separate property) and are left unmapped. All used class and property URIs appear verbatim in the provided ontology.

